WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:05.451
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:05.517 --> 00:08.852
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.919 --> 00:11.986
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:12.070 --> 00:14.971
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:15.022 --> 00:18.939
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.990 --> 00:21.407
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do... ♪

00:21.474 --> 00:22.990
Uh, uh, hold it.
Hold it.

00:23.074 --> 00:24.657
What-what...
what's going on here?

00:24.742 --> 00:25.774
I'm pregnant.

00:25.842 --> 00:27.025
You...
you're what?

00:27.110 --> 00:28.193
Yeah.

00:28.278 --> 00:29.861
We're in the middle
of a thing here.

00:29.945 --> 00:31.444
Well, you didn't return
any of my texts.

00:31.495 --> 00:32.828
So what-what...
so what's going on?

00:32.912 --> 00:34.962
You-you... you want, like,
a... a ride to the place?

00:35.029 --> 00:36.329
Peter, what's
going on?

00:36.396 --> 00:38.929
What's going on, Lois,
is that this girl

00:38.997 --> 00:41.713
is obviously not well,
and I have just learned

00:41.781 --> 00:43.347
that she's been stealing
from the show,

00:43.415 --> 00:46.749
and she should probably
be escorted out of the building.

00:50.918 --> 00:52.668
♪ Nah, nah ♪

00:52.753 --> 00:55.618
♪ Family Guy. ♪

00:58.889 --> 01:00.588
ANNOUNCER (over TV):
We now return

01:00.656 --> 01:03.722
<i>to Star Trek II: The Wrath
of Khan, edited for goats.</i>

01:03.790 --> 01:06.623
KHAN (over speaker):
Marooned for all eternity

01:06.691 --> 01:09.374
in the center of a dead planet.

01:10.877 --> 01:12.959
Buried alive.

01:13.010 --> 01:15.826
Buried alive.

01:15.878 --> 01:17.962
(bleating)

01:18.013 --> 01:20.163
Lois, I need $28,000.

01:20.229 --> 01:21.430
For what?

01:21.497 --> 01:23.413
I've decided I want to open
a sushi restaurant.

01:23.481 --> 01:24.615
What do you know
about sushi?

01:24.682 --> 01:26.098
I don't care
about the sushi.

01:26.166 --> 01:28.582
I just want to yell at customers
when they walk in the door.

01:28.650 --> 01:31.650
(speaking faux Japanese)

01:33.518 --> 01:35.935
Here comes a black guy!

01:36.002 --> 01:37.152
Peter, you're not
getting money

01:37.220 --> 01:38.519
for that or
anything else.

01:38.587 --> 01:40.685
We're running seriously
low on cash right now.

01:40.770 --> 01:43.355
In fact, I may have to
get my own full-time job.

01:43.439 --> 01:45.571
Geez. Are we really living
that close to the edge?

01:45.638 --> 01:46.854
You know we
are, Brian.

01:46.922 --> 01:48.589
Why do you think
we waited so long

01:48.658 --> 01:50.290
to take you to the
groomer's last month?

01:53.675 --> 01:55.876
Hey, isn't it time for me
to get a haircut?

01:55.945 --> 01:58.176
Oh, no. I think
you just got one.

01:58.244 --> 01:59.510
Are you sure?

01:59.578 --> 02:01.511
'Cause I feel like
my nails are really long.

02:01.579 --> 02:03.211
Yeah, I'll check,
but I'm pretty sure

02:03.280 --> 02:04.963
it's not for another
couple of weeks.

02:05.031 --> 02:06.463
(panting)

02:06.530 --> 02:08.098
What day is this?

02:08.165 --> 02:10.163
Like it or not, we're
going to have to start

02:10.231 --> 02:11.864
living on a strict
budget for a while.

02:11.932 --> 02:13.667
Good evening. I'm Tom Tucker,
and this is...

02:13.735 --> 02:15.133
♪ News... ♪

02:15.201 --> 02:17.217
I thought you were going
to sing with me, Joyce.

02:17.285 --> 02:18.484
Okay. Our top
story tonight.

02:18.552 --> 02:20.118
The Rhode Island
State Lottery

02:20.186 --> 02:23.286
has climbed to a record
$150 million jackpot.

02:23.354 --> 02:24.653
That's right, Tom.

02:24.721 --> 02:26.855
Powerball fever has
officially gripped Quahog.

02:26.923 --> 02:29.388
We sent Channel Five News
Asian correspondent

02:29.456 --> 02:32.357
Tricia Takanawa around town
to get the public reaction.

02:32.424 --> 02:35.758
Mayor West, what do you plan
to do if you win the lottery?

02:35.826 --> 02:37.959
Well, I'd finally
splurge and buy myself

02:38.025 --> 02:40.659
one of those fancy
four-piece suits.

02:40.727 --> 02:42.593
I'd like to join
your country club.

02:42.660 --> 02:44.994
I assume that won't
be a problem.

02:45.062 --> 02:48.628
And how about you,
every Persian guy in the world?

02:48.697 --> 02:50.964
ALL:
White BMW.

02:51.031 --> 02:52.031
There you go, Lois.

02:52.098 --> 02:53.198
That's the answer right there.

02:53.249 --> 02:54.799
We'll just win the lottery.

02:54.865 --> 02:57.365
In fact, I'm going to go buy
my winning ticket right now.

03:02.252 --> 03:03.419
Peter, what's wrong?

03:03.487 --> 03:04.585
It's, uh, nothing.

03:04.653 --> 03:06.502
I'm just going
to wait a minute.

03:06.570 --> 03:09.003
There's teenagers
in skinny jeans out there.

03:12.606 --> 03:15.273
Everyone, I got big news.

03:15.341 --> 03:18.208
We are going
to be rich.

03:18.276 --> 03:19.758
Oh, my God, Peter.

03:19.825 --> 03:21.759
There are thousands of
lottery tickets here.

03:21.826 --> 03:23.759
Where did you get the
money for all these?

03:23.827 --> 03:26.093
Simple. I took out a second
mortgage on the house.

03:26.161 --> 03:27.978
What?!
Peter, this is idiotic.

03:28.045 --> 03:30.095
Your odds of winning
are like 100 million to one.

03:30.163 --> 03:33.030
Don't you know the lottery
is just a tax on stupid people?

03:33.097 --> 03:34.680
Would you be saying
that if the prize

03:34.748 --> 03:37.049
was 150 million bags of
the neighbor's garbage?

03:37.117 --> 03:38.148
But it's not.

03:38.216 --> 03:39.515
I mean... I mean, is it?

03:39.583 --> 03:41.700
It's... that-that is an
unrealistic, unfair question.

03:41.768 --> 03:43.050
Peter, Brian's right.

03:43.118 --> 03:44.949
How can you be
so irresponsible?

03:45.017 --> 03:46.601
You take these tickets
back right now.

03:46.669 --> 03:47.635
No way, Lois.

03:47.703 --> 03:48.835
We're going to win.

03:48.903 --> 03:50.536
I got lots of good
karma built up

03:50.604 --> 03:51.987
from doing those
USO shows.

03:52.054 --> 03:53.238
(laughter)

03:53.322 --> 03:54.938
So, apparently, they found

03:55.005 --> 03:57.072
a weapon of mass destruction--
my putter.

03:57.139 --> 03:59.806
(laughter)

03:59.874 --> 04:01.523
All right, boys.
Go get 'em over there.

04:01.591 --> 04:04.424
And now, please welcome
Mr. Bruce Jenner.

04:04.492 --> 04:05.841
(cheering and applause)

04:05.909 --> 04:08.492
(jazzy burlesque music plays)

04:08.559 --> 04:11.127
(sailors cheering and whistling)

04:21.416 --> 04:23.065
Just wanted to
remind you fellas

04:23.116 --> 04:24.966
what you're all
fighting for.

04:28.485 --> 04:29.935
Okay, this is
it, you guys.

04:30.003 --> 04:31.470
They're going to announce
the winning lottery numbers.

04:31.537 --> 04:34.603
Time for the Griffin family
to meet its destiny.

04:34.671 --> 04:35.638
When we lose,
I'm getting a divorce.

04:35.704 --> 04:37.171
Understood.

04:37.239 --> 04:39.206
We're here live in our studio
where a checkout girl

04:39.273 --> 04:41.305
in a cheap dress
who wants to be a model

04:41.373 --> 04:42.889
is ready to draw
the winning numbers.

04:42.958 --> 04:44.256
"E!"

04:44.340 --> 04:45.290
TUCKER: That's
a three, honey.

04:45.375 --> 04:46.374
Turn it around.

04:46.425 --> 04:47.374
This.

04:47.425 --> 04:48.424
Thirty-one.

04:48.508 --> 04:49.842
Spooky ghost mouth.

04:49.909 --> 04:51.075
That's a zero.

04:51.126 --> 04:52.476
Snowman.

04:52.543 --> 04:53.592
Eight.

04:53.677 --> 04:55.011
Two sailboats.

04:55.078 --> 04:56.262
Forty-four.

04:56.346 --> 04:57.679
Seventeen.

04:57.746 --> 04:59.263
No, that's...
Oh, yes, that is 17.

04:59.346 --> 05:00.796
She's good at 17.

05:00.880 --> 05:02.180
Well, folks,
those are our winning numbers.

05:02.248 --> 05:04.285
Good luck to all of our...

05:04.286 --> 05:05.250
Twenty-four!

05:05.251 --> 05:06.322
No, that's it. We're done.

05:06.323 --> 05:07.861
Anyone watching,
do not count 24.

05:07.862 --> 05:09.184
Pancakes!

05:09.185 --> 05:10.098
Okay, I don't know
what she's doing now.

05:10.099 --> 05:11.298
Somebody turn the machine off.

05:11.328 --> 05:12.427
N-Nikki, Nikki, that's good.
That's good.

05:12.462 --> 05:13.428
Go... go wait in my car.

05:13.451 --> 05:15.352
All right, you did good.

05:16.300 --> 05:18.833
Going to take her
to Walt's Roast Beef.

05:18.901 --> 05:20.368
All right, kids,
we got 200,000

05:20.435 --> 05:21.668
lottery tickets
we got to check.

05:21.736 --> 05:23.535
All right, how do you
want to do this?

05:23.602 --> 05:25.869
A static shot of the house
where night turns into day

05:25.937 --> 05:28.437
or a montage song that
over-explains what we're doing?

05:28.505 --> 05:30.305
Um, that
second one

05:30.373 --> 05:31.871
sounds like it
could be funny.

05:31.939 --> 05:33.323
♪ ♪

05:33.407 --> 05:35.023
♪ Checking lottery tickets ♪

05:35.108 --> 05:36.873
♪ Looking at the numbers ♪

05:36.941 --> 05:39.641
♪ And seeing if they match
the ones on the news ♪

05:39.693 --> 05:41.326
♪ If they do,
then we're winners ♪

05:41.410 --> 05:43.376
♪ If they don't,
then we put them ♪

05:43.443 --> 05:45.160
♪ In the designated pile ♪

05:45.228 --> 05:47.176
♪ For tickets
we already checked ♪

05:47.244 --> 05:49.443
♪ This is taking forever ♪

05:49.511 --> 05:51.628
♪ That's why Brian is yawning ♪

05:51.696 --> 05:53.830
♪ And Stewie's
rubbing his eyes ♪

05:53.898 --> 05:56.414
♪ The montage is almost over ♪

05:56.481 --> 05:58.831
♪ That's why the music
and the vocals ♪

05:58.916 --> 06:02.482
♪ Are fading out right now. ♪

06:02.534 --> 06:04.034
Well, that's it.

06:04.101 --> 06:07.268
Three days wasted looking
through 200,000 lottery tickets.

06:07.336 --> 06:08.735
Those weren't
the lottery tickets.

06:08.803 --> 06:10.270
That was a test.

06:10.337 --> 06:12.288
These are
the lottery tickets.

06:14.172 --> 06:15.855
Is this another test?

06:15.923 --> 06:17.156
Yes.

06:17.224 --> 06:18.757
These aren't the real
lottery tickets, either.

06:18.824 --> 06:21.174
These are the real
lottery tickets.

06:21.258 --> 06:24.625
So, in addition to buying
200,000 lottery tickets,

06:24.693 --> 06:27.092
you had 400,000 fake ones
printed up?

06:27.160 --> 06:29.961
I had to be sure.

06:31.145 --> 06:33.311
Oh, my God. We won!

06:33.379 --> 06:34.696
What?
Let me see that.

06:36.132 --> 06:37.896
Yes! We won the lottery!

06:37.964 --> 06:39.297
I'm getting a penis butler.

06:39.348 --> 06:40.481
Sir?

06:40.549 --> 06:42.365
All right, butle
my penis. Butle it!

06:47.840 --> 06:48.906
This is awesome.

06:48.973 --> 06:50.138
Now that we're rich,

06:50.206 --> 06:51.973
our lives are going
to be so much better.

06:52.041 --> 06:53.608
You sure it was
such a good idea

06:53.676 --> 06:55.076
to cash that
lottery check?

06:55.142 --> 06:57.476
It seems foolish to have
all this money lying around.

06:57.544 --> 06:59.410
Oh, you'd rather have
it down at the bank

06:59.478 --> 07:01.012
where the Jewish
guys can leer at it?

07:01.079 --> 07:02.694
Yeah!
All right!

07:02.762 --> 07:03.796
Money!
(whistling)

07:03.863 --> 07:05.195
Whoo-hoo!
Whoo!

07:05.263 --> 07:07.497
(phone rings)
Hello?

07:07.564 --> 07:09.263
CARTER: Did you blow all your money yet?
No, Daddy.

07:09.331 --> 07:10.831
All right. Call me
when you blow all your money.

07:10.899 --> 07:12.299
Love you. Bye.

07:12.367 --> 07:14.033
All right, so how are we
going to spend our winnings?

07:14.100 --> 07:15.633
I'm going to get some
supermarket fried chicken

07:15.701 --> 07:17.633
and eat it until I'm nauseous.

07:17.718 --> 07:20.518
I'm going to finally get my gal
that doodad she's been wantin'!

07:20.586 --> 07:22.619
I'm going to get a floor mirror
to squat over

07:22.687 --> 07:24.487
and see what's making
all that noise.

07:24.555 --> 07:26.287
Okay, everybody
just calm down.

07:26.354 --> 07:28.154
We're not going to go crazy
spending our winnings,

07:28.222 --> 07:30.189
and we're not going to let
this money change us.

07:30.256 --> 07:31.657
What are you talking about?

07:31.725 --> 07:34.023
This money is our ticket
to the good life, starting now.

07:34.091 --> 07:36.558
I just bought a giant room
full of gold coins,

07:36.625 --> 07:39.059
and I'm going to dive into it
like Scrooge McDuck.

07:39.127 --> 07:40.660
(drumroll)

07:42.694 --> 07:45.361
Aah! It's not a liquid!

07:45.429 --> 07:48.662
It's a great many pieces
of solid matter

07:48.730 --> 07:51.196
that form a hard,
floor-like surface!

07:51.263 --> 07:52.629
Aah!

07:56.884 --> 07:58.948
Peter, what are you
doing here?

07:59.016 --> 08:00.382
Shouldn't you
be at work?

08:00.450 --> 08:01.884
I quit my job, Lois.

08:01.951 --> 08:03.585
You what?
Yeah, we're rich now.

08:03.652 --> 08:04.984
I don't need to be working.

08:05.053 --> 08:06.218
Ah, you should've
been there, Lois.

08:06.286 --> 08:08.320
I told Angela what she could do
with that job,

08:08.388 --> 08:10.287
just like I always fantasized.

08:10.355 --> 08:11.921
Angela, I just want
to thank you

08:11.989 --> 08:14.022
for several extremely
pleasurable years

08:14.090 --> 08:15.323
working for this
corporation.

08:15.391 --> 08:17.623
Uh, certain
unexpected developments

08:17.690 --> 08:19.223
have created
a situation

08:19.291 --> 08:21.459
where I am no longer
in need of employment.

08:21.527 --> 08:23.293
Uh, I would be
remiss, however,

08:23.361 --> 08:25.593
if I did not extend
my gratitude to you

08:25.661 --> 08:28.095
for your unwavering
fairness and belief in me,

08:28.163 --> 08:30.262
and there is a giant poo
on your desk.

08:33.631 --> 08:35.447
Hey, Horace,
another round for everybody.

08:35.498 --> 08:38.165
(patrons cheering)

08:38.232 --> 08:39.382
Gosh, Peter,
you're really

08:39.450 --> 08:40.517
being generous
with your money.

08:40.585 --> 08:42.051
Hey, what's the point
of being rich

08:42.119 --> 08:43.950
if you can't share it
with your pals, huh?

08:44.017 --> 08:45.234
Okay, wait,
I-I got one.

08:45.302 --> 08:46.636
Who would you
rather do?

08:46.703 --> 08:48.468
Susan Boyle
or a sex doll

08:48.536 --> 08:50.487
that's been passed
around a fraternity?

08:50.554 --> 08:51.954
Well, have they been
cleaning the doll?

08:52.021 --> 08:54.204
Please, these guys are Betas--
they're total animals.

08:54.272 --> 08:55.337
Hey, I got
one, Peter.

08:55.405 --> 08:56.538
What would you
rather do?

08:56.605 --> 08:58.173
Tread water where
you are right now

08:58.240 --> 09:00.006
or increase your
net worth fivefold

09:00.074 --> 09:01.339
within the next
18 months?

09:01.407 --> 09:02.473
Huh?

09:02.541 --> 09:04.075
What? I... what are you
talking about?

09:04.142 --> 09:06.208
Well, my friend Marty
is a whiz with chemistry,

09:06.276 --> 09:07.675
and he says
he's come up

09:07.743 --> 09:09.259
with a new penis
enlargement pill,

09:09.326 --> 09:12.377
and if we can bankroll him
$100,000 in startup money,

09:12.446 --> 09:14.279
he'll triple our
investment in a year.

09:14.346 --> 09:16.914
Well, uh...
gee, I-I'm... I don't, uh...

09:16.981 --> 09:18.313
Peter, I've taken
the liberty

09:18.381 --> 09:20.247
of mocking up some
sales projections.

09:20.315 --> 09:21.748
Wow, that's pretty good.

09:21.816 --> 09:23.749
And this would be
our net profits.

09:23.817 --> 09:24.949
Yikes.

09:25.017 --> 09:26.450
You know, Peter, uh,

09:26.517 --> 09:29.784
Bonnie's birthday is coming up,
and I'll be gol-darned

09:29.852 --> 09:32.585
if I didn't go ahead and
promise her that Stevie Nicks

09:32.653 --> 09:35.587
would come to our house
and sing three songs to her.

09:35.655 --> 09:38.922
(chuckling):
Boy, do I got a big mouth, huh?

09:38.989 --> 09:41.189
Yeah, if you're going to open
your mouth with the missus,

09:41.256 --> 09:42.457
stick to kisses, huh?
(laughs)

09:42.524 --> 09:45.558
(both laughing)

09:45.625 --> 09:47.759
What neat humor.
What neat humor.

09:47.827 --> 09:50.093
So what do say, Peter?
Can you help us out?

09:50.160 --> 09:51.560
Of course I can, you dope.

09:51.627 --> 09:53.276
We're pals, right?

09:55.894 --> 09:57.043
There you go.

09:57.111 --> 09:58.079
Hey, Horace,
give us

09:58.146 --> 09:59.378
three shots
of your best bourbon.

09:59.446 --> 10:01.763
And have that Russian waiter
I like bring it over.

10:01.831 --> 10:03.479
(jaunty intro plays)

10:03.530 --> 10:06.014
♪ La la la la la
la la la la la la ♪

10:06.065 --> 10:08.365
♪ Trololo ♪

10:08.450 --> 10:10.849
♪ La la la la la
la la la la la la ♪

10:10.916 --> 10:12.066
♪ Ho ho ho ho ho ♪

10:12.134 --> 10:13.201
♪ Ho ho ho ho ho ♪

10:13.285 --> 10:14.484
♪ Ho ho ho ho ho ♪

10:14.552 --> 10:15.851
♪ Ho ho ho ho ho ♪

10:15.919 --> 10:18.353
♪ La la la la la la
la la la la la la ♪

10:18.420 --> 10:20.837
♪ La la la la la
la la la la la ♪

10:20.904 --> 10:25.706
♪ Ah la la la ♪

10:25.774 --> 10:30.308
♪ La la la la la la ♪

10:30.375 --> 10:32.409
♪ La la la la la ♪

10:32.477 --> 10:35.210
♪ Bop ba da da da da da da da ♪

10:35.277 --> 10:40.178
♪ La la la la la la. ♪

10:40.246 --> 10:42.178
Boy, you wouldn't guess
from that smile

10:42.246 --> 10:44.047
that all his kids
were stillborn, huh?

10:44.115 --> 10:46.348
All right, well,
thanks again, Peter.

10:46.416 --> 10:47.815
I got to head out.

10:47.883 --> 10:49.782
I told my mother I'd visit
her in the hospital.

10:49.850 --> 10:51.233
Yeah, and I got to work.

10:51.301 --> 10:52.700
I'm flying a leg
to Minneapolis tonight.

10:52.768 --> 10:54.034
Oh.

10:54.085 --> 10:55.334
Yeah, I-I-I understand.

10:55.402 --> 10:56.601
You... you've got
other things to do.

10:56.668 --> 10:58.201
I-I just wrote you guys
a couple of checks,

10:58.269 --> 10:59.885
but no, you... you-you-you
do your things.

10:59.953 --> 11:01.718
What?
No, I mean, I-I'm just saying,

11:01.786 --> 11:04.120
I-I thought friends hang out,
do things with each other.

11:04.187 --> 11:05.737
You know,
especially when one friend

11:05.805 --> 11:08.155
gives the other friends
a whole bunch of money.

11:08.223 --> 11:11.739
Oh. Well, I guess I
could stick around.

11:11.807 --> 11:14.841
Yeah, and I-I guess
the co-pilot can fly the plane.

11:14.909 --> 11:17.743
She's a woman and passengers
are usually okay with that.

11:17.811 --> 11:19.109
I once saw a
deodorant commercial

11:19.177 --> 11:20.442
that had a
woman pilot.

11:20.510 --> 11:22.061
That's more like it.

11:22.145 --> 11:24.578
From now on, we're all gonna do
whatever I want to do.

11:24.646 --> 11:25.928
'Cause I'm the one
with the dough.

11:25.996 --> 11:27.630
Okay, I guess that's fair.

11:27.698 --> 11:29.363
Good.
'Cause what I want to do is

11:29.431 --> 11:31.431
stay here, get drunk
and watch TV.

11:31.499 --> 11:34.600
<i>ANNOUNCER: We now return to Michael
McDonald During Allergy Season.</i>

11:34.667 --> 11:36.532
(gasping):
Haa...

11:36.600 --> 11:38.850
Haa-haa...

11:38.919 --> 11:40.935
Haa-haa-haa-haa-haa...

11:41.020 --> 11:42.402
Haa...

11:42.487 --> 11:43.886
(sneezes):
♪ Haa-chu! ♪

11:46.021 --> 11:48.788
Hey, Peter, you wanted me
to watch the entire first season

11:48.856 --> 11:51.389
<i>of True Blood and come show you
where there was boob?</i>

11:51.455 --> 11:52.856
That's right, I
don't want to see

11:52.924 --> 11:54.190
<i>any actual True
Blood, just boob.</i>

11:54.257 --> 11:55.456
Yeah, well, I found some.

11:58.308 --> 11:59.542
No!

11:59.610 --> 12:02.008
Anna Paquin boob does
not count as real boob!

12:02.076 --> 12:04.243
That's like looking
at a 12-year-old boy.

12:04.310 --> 12:06.344
Geez. Y-You should
know that, Joe!

12:06.410 --> 12:07.445
You should
definitely know that!

12:07.513 --> 12:08.812
Sorry, Peter.

12:08.879 --> 12:10.812
Sorry doesn't show me
nipples worth seeing!

12:10.880 --> 12:13.647
And as for you, Anna, you're
dreadful and so is the program.

12:13.715 --> 12:14.847
I didn't write it.

12:14.915 --> 12:16.547
Yeah, but you know
the people who do.

12:16.615 --> 12:18.149
That Alan Ball--
you know him, right?

12:18.216 --> 12:19.182
Yeah.

12:19.250 --> 12:20.282
How come everything
he writes

12:20.350 --> 12:22.183
is so dreary?
I don't know.

12:22.251 --> 12:23.917
Tell him to cheer up!
Things ain't so bad!

12:23.985 --> 12:26.384
This is a weird episode.

12:28.186 --> 12:30.420
Okay, Quagmire, I want you
to take the first bite

12:30.488 --> 12:31.787
out of this fudge pop,

12:31.855 --> 12:34.220
because that first bite
is the one that really hurts.

12:34.288 --> 12:36.656
Well, okay.

12:36.724 --> 12:40.240
(grunting)

12:40.307 --> 12:42.442
Ah! Ow!

12:42.509 --> 12:43.442
Ow!

12:43.510 --> 12:44.509
Yeah, that's right "Ow."

12:44.576 --> 12:45.609
Now, you swallow that.

12:45.677 --> 12:46.909
Don't you dare spit that out.

12:46.977 --> 12:47.860
What's the
difference?

12:47.928 --> 12:48.777
You got what
you wanted.

12:48.845 --> 12:49.794
I said swallow it!

12:49.862 --> 12:50.979
(gulps)

12:51.047 --> 12:52.512
Now, smear what's
left of it on your face

12:52.580 --> 12:56.429
and look at me
with your mouth open.

12:56.498 --> 12:58.632
Look at yourself,
you filthy whore.

13:01.133 --> 13:02.932
I don't even
want this now.

13:07.468 --> 13:10.101
Okay, guys, now you're gonna do
a synchronized duet

13:10.169 --> 13:12.669
of "Makin' Whoopee" while
I shoot you with this BB gun.

13:12.737 --> 13:13.702
That doesn't
sound safe.

13:13.770 --> 13:14.537
Action!

13:14.604 --> 13:16.055
(piano playing)

13:16.104 --> 13:17.854
♪ Another bride ♪
♪ She looks so gay ♪

13:17.921 --> 13:19.555
♪ Another June ♪
♪ Oh, what a day ♪

13:19.606 --> 13:22.290
♪ Another sunny
honeymoon ♪

13:22.356 --> 13:23.490
♪ Hip hip hoor... ♪
(screams)

13:23.558 --> 13:25.057
♪ Another seas... ♪
(screams)

13:25.124 --> 13:26.758
♪ Another reason ♪

13:26.826 --> 13:28.624
♪ For making whoopee ♪

13:30.760 --> 13:33.311
Look, you aren't gonna shoot us
in the eye, are you, Peter?

13:33.395 --> 13:34.444
Oh, no, Joe.

13:34.529 --> 13:36.112
I wouldn't try
to shoot you in the eye.

13:36.196 --> 13:38.146
♪ A lot of shoes ♪
♪ We're throwing shoes ♪

13:38.231 --> 13:39.636
♪ A lot of rice ♪
♪ It's all so ni... ♪

13:39.661 --> 13:40.581
(screams)

13:40.582 --> 13:43.080
Peter, it was really
close to my eye!

13:43.148 --> 13:44.081
Relax, Joe.

13:44.149 --> 13:45.349
I'm nowhere near your eye.

13:45.417 --> 13:46.816
Hey, Joe, open your eye.

13:46.884 --> 13:49.651
(screaming)
Gotcha.

13:49.717 --> 13:51.618
What the hell, Peter?!
You shot him in the eye!

13:51.686 --> 13:53.085
Keep singing, you.
And keep your chin up,

13:53.153 --> 13:54.319
so I can see your throat.

13:54.403 --> 13:56.102
No, no screw this!
You're a jerk!

13:56.170 --> 13:57.904
Jerk? What kind of a way is that

13:57.972 --> 13:59.737
to talk to your friend
who gives you money?

13:59.805 --> 14:00.838
Go to hell, Peter!

14:00.906 --> 14:02.038
We don't need
your money!

14:02.106 --> 14:03.538
And we don't need
friends like you!

14:03.606 --> 14:04.905
Yeah! We're out of here!

14:04.973 --> 14:06.172
Fine, go on. I don't care.

14:06.240 --> 14:08.006
I don't need you, I got money!

14:08.074 --> 14:10.342
You know, Peter,
you used to be a great guy.

14:10.409 --> 14:11.942
But ever since
you won that lottery...

14:12.010 --> 14:13.408
(screaming)

14:13.476 --> 14:15.209
Come on, Joe!

14:15.277 --> 14:17.010
(bleep) this guy!
Aah!

14:20.312 --> 14:22.379
<i>I'm here to audition
for Fiddler on the Roof.</i>

14:22.447 --> 14:23.779
Come right in.

14:32.240 --> 14:34.540
Peter, my God, what the
hell are you wearing?

14:34.607 --> 14:36.658
It's a solid gold
tuxedo, Lois.

14:36.726 --> 14:38.126
I had to fight three rappers

14:38.193 --> 14:40.025
down at the Nonsense Store
for this.

14:40.093 --> 14:41.826
Look, Peter, this
is not who we are.

14:41.894 --> 14:43.927
I'm worried the money
is changing this family,

14:43.995 --> 14:45.428
and not the
way you hoped.

14:45.496 --> 14:47.762
Well, I was hoping it would make
you shut up, so you're right.

14:47.831 --> 14:49.562
You know, I spoke to
Bonnie this morning,

14:49.630 --> 14:52.531
and she said you haven't talked
to Joe or Quagmire in two weeks.

14:52.599 --> 14:54.082
Who cares?
I don't need Quagmire.

14:54.149 --> 14:55.781
And I certainly don't need Joe.

14:55.849 --> 14:57.082
I got Money Joe.

14:57.150 --> 14:58.066
(laughs)

14:58.135 --> 14:59.466
That's a good one, Money Joe!

14:59.535 --> 15:01.635
I don't like what this money
is doing to us.

15:01.703 --> 15:03.734
It's even affecting
Brian and Stewie.

15:03.802 --> 15:04.869
(yawns)

15:04.936 --> 15:06.035
I think I'm gonna
take a nap.

15:06.103 --> 15:08.235
What, here?
Yeah, I had a giant mobile

15:08.302 --> 15:10.653
put in the sky so I can
sleep wherever I want.

15:10.720 --> 15:13.171
(mobile playing soft music)

15:13.255 --> 15:15.506
(sighs)
This is nice.

15:15.590 --> 15:17.823
I hope that bear
comes around soon.

15:17.875 --> 15:20.425
That's sort of my fave--
Oh, look, the bear!

15:20.492 --> 15:21.941
And it's not just them.

15:22.009 --> 15:24.910
Chris has also been developing
some very expensive tastes.

15:24.978 --> 15:27.243
Model glue, sir?

15:27.310 --> 15:28.961
Well, what do
we have today?

15:29.012 --> 15:32.112
Today we have a recent vintage
procured just this morning

15:32.180 --> 15:33.863
from Michael's Hobby Shop.

15:33.931 --> 15:35.930
Yes, that's
very nice.

15:35.997 --> 15:37.898
Bring me my
finest rag.

15:37.966 --> 15:39.715
Your fancy rag, sir.

15:39.799 --> 15:41.566
You are relieved
for the evening.

15:43.234 --> 15:46.751
(inhales deeply)

15:46.819 --> 15:49.820
You don't see
poor people doing this!

15:49.888 --> 15:51.053
Lois, don't you understand?

15:51.138 --> 15:53.319
We don't got any of
our old problems anymore.

15:53.387 --> 15:55.256
We don't have to worry
about paying bills,

15:55.323 --> 15:57.139
we don't have to worry
about saving dough.

15:57.207 --> 15:59.606
All we got to do
is enjoy ourselves.

15:59.674 --> 16:00.689
Interested?

16:01.842 --> 16:03.408
(gasps)

16:03.475 --> 16:05.858
Oh, my God.

16:05.943 --> 16:09.192
Peter, it's
beautiful!

16:09.260 --> 16:10.492
Is it a blood
diamond?

16:10.560 --> 16:11.927
Ah, the bloodiest.

16:11.994 --> 16:14.595
The two kids who found it were
forced to murder each other.

16:14.663 --> 16:16.429
Oh, Peter,
I love it.

16:16.497 --> 16:19.363
Hey, you want to watch a DVD
of the murder while we do it?

16:19.447 --> 16:20.647
I already watched it
eight times,

16:20.715 --> 16:22.847
so I know exactly
which part I want to blam at.

16:26.151 --> 16:28.483
Chris, Meg, now that
I'm a rich father,

16:28.551 --> 16:30.984
you will try to impress me,
and I will remain distant.

16:31.052 --> 16:32.085
Go.

16:32.153 --> 16:34.185
Um, say, Dad,
did I tell you

16:34.253 --> 16:36.354
I got second place
in the yacht race?

16:36.422 --> 16:38.621
Hmm. Sounds like
somebody's dad

16:38.689 --> 16:40.287
is happier than
me right now.

16:40.355 --> 16:41.988
Well, um, how about this, Dad?

16:42.056 --> 16:44.122
The school paper is
doing a story about me.

16:44.190 --> 16:45.924
That's because I
had it arranged.

16:45.992 --> 16:48.758
Uh, I was thinking about
joining the Army?

16:48.826 --> 16:50.025
No.

16:50.093 --> 16:52.026
Meg, as a girl, your
life holds no merit.

16:52.094 --> 16:54.260
And, Chris, try as you
may, you will never be

16:54.328 --> 16:56.127
as good as your older
brother who died.

16:56.195 --> 16:57.828
He was good at
sports and talking.

16:57.879 --> 16:59.747
Now, pardon me
while I look at scrimshaw

16:59.814 --> 17:01.513
through a magnifying glass.

17:01.581 --> 17:04.014
Ah, brave men all.

17:04.082 --> 17:05.982
Lost sons of New Bedford.

17:06.050 --> 17:07.650
That's good scrimshaw.

17:10.918 --> 17:13.317
Ah, I never thought
I'd be having high tea

17:13.385 --> 17:15.584
at the Park Barrington
every single day.

17:15.652 --> 17:18.352
Yes, it's nice to eat in a sea
of white faces, isn't it?

17:18.420 --> 17:21.687
Waiter, have the chef prepare
a bucket of your finest caviar,

17:21.755 --> 17:23.738
and then give it
a helicopter tour of the city.

17:23.806 --> 17:24.772
Very good, sir.

17:24.840 --> 17:26.306
Very good indeed.

17:26.373 --> 17:29.274
I hope it enjoys it as much as
that Maine lobster I ordered.

17:29.342 --> 17:31.740
And over there is the
Rhode Island State House,

17:31.808 --> 17:34.008
the second largest
self-supporting marble dome

17:34.076 --> 17:35.443
in North America.

17:35.510 --> 17:37.010
Why the (bleep)
am I doing this?

17:37.078 --> 17:38.611
I could've just
said I did it.

17:38.678 --> 17:40.344
Sir, about
your bill...

17:40.395 --> 17:41.945
Oh, don't worry, Carstairs,

17:42.013 --> 17:44.079
there's ample gratuity
on there for you.

17:44.146 --> 17:45.446
(chuckles):
Nyah.

17:45.513 --> 17:48.249
Well, actually, sir, your
card has been declined.

17:48.316 --> 17:49.682
(laughs):
Oh, really?

17:49.750 --> 17:52.098
Well, I'm sure a brief call
to my accountant

17:52.166 --> 17:54.301
will resolve this
to our mutual satisfaction.

17:56.267 --> 17:57.534
Hello, Schnozzenstein?

17:57.602 --> 17:58.935
This is Peter Griffin.

17:59.002 --> 18:00.936
There seems to be
some issue with our credit.

18:01.003 --> 18:03.803
I told Carstairs
you'd set everything straight.

18:03.871 --> 18:05.304
What do you mean I'm broke?!

18:05.371 --> 18:07.655
What about all that money
I sent to the Gayman Islands?

18:07.723 --> 18:09.456
They did what with it?!

18:09.523 --> 18:11.557
No, I don't want it back!

18:11.625 --> 18:13.274
I'm gonna miss you,
Carstairs.

18:13.341 --> 18:15.808
I shall miss
you, too, sir.

18:15.876 --> 18:17.491
With no one to look after,

18:17.558 --> 18:19.326
Carstairs fell into
a deep depression

18:19.394 --> 18:21.094
from which he never emerged.

18:21.159 --> 18:22.926
He died two weeks later.

18:22.994 --> 18:24.561
Some say of a broken heart.

18:24.630 --> 18:25.562
What, sir?

18:25.630 --> 18:26.862
But that was Carstairs.

18:30.430 --> 18:31.914
I can't believe it.

18:31.983 --> 18:33.248
We lost everything.

18:33.316 --> 18:34.848
What do we do now?

18:34.916 --> 18:37.116
Well, seems like our
only hope is the lottery.

18:37.184 --> 18:40.250
Holy (bleep), we won twice and
we're right back here again.

18:40.318 --> 18:43.686
We had $150 million, and
we blew through it in a month.

18:43.753 --> 18:45.870
Yeah, but on the bright side,
if this hadn't happened,

18:45.938 --> 18:47.004
we never would've met Kyle.

18:47.071 --> 18:48.670
Hey.

18:48.738 --> 18:49.705
Hello. Hey there.
Hi. What's up?

18:49.772 --> 18:51.272
And that's not even Kyle.

18:51.339 --> 18:52.805
Peter, we can't
live like this.

18:52.873 --> 18:54.523
Well, what are we
supposed to do, Lois?

18:54.591 --> 18:55.724
Everything we
had is gone!

18:55.792 --> 18:57.340
We have friends, Peter.

18:57.408 --> 18:58.624
Not anymore, we don't.

18:58.692 --> 18:59.858
They're good people.

18:59.926 --> 19:00.958
They'll listen.

19:01.025 --> 19:02.659
And they'll care
what happens to us,

19:02.728 --> 19:05.427
even after everything
that's happened.

19:05.494 --> 19:07.728
You know, I'm still young
enough you can drop me

19:07.796 --> 19:09.562
at the fire station,
no questions asked.

19:17.632 --> 19:19.931
Well, well.
Look who it is.

19:19.999 --> 19:20.899
Who is it?

19:20.967 --> 19:22.017
It's me, Peter.

19:22.084 --> 19:22.950
I'm standing
right here.

19:23.018 --> 19:24.450
This is a glass eye, Peter.

19:24.518 --> 19:26.218
They had to remove
the one you shot.

19:26.286 --> 19:28.685
Oh, boy, that whole
day was a mess, huh?

19:28.753 --> 19:30.452
What happened to you, anyway?
You look terrible.

19:30.521 --> 19:32.887
Look, you guys,
I messed up bad.

19:32.955 --> 19:34.887
Winning the lottery
was the worst thing

19:34.956 --> 19:36.789
that ever happened
to me and my family.

19:36.857 --> 19:39.324
I thought being rich
would solve all my problems,

19:39.392 --> 19:42.424
but all it did was make me
forget what was important.

19:42.492 --> 19:44.557
Like who my real friends are.

19:44.609 --> 19:46.309
I don't expect
you to forgive me,

19:46.394 --> 19:49.893
but if, for some reason, you do,
you can find me

19:49.944 --> 19:52.595
in a cardboard box on the corner
of Meeting and Thayer.

19:54.414 --> 19:55.647
Peter, wait.

19:57.314 --> 19:59.197
That corner's
a bit of a hike for me.

19:59.265 --> 20:00.780
I'd rather just
come see you at home.

20:03.333 --> 20:05.633
What-- where'd you get
this kind of money?

20:05.701 --> 20:08.200
The penis enlargement pill you
gave me the startup money for.

20:08.268 --> 20:09.718
It turned a pretty nice profit.

20:09.785 --> 20:12.152
I guess it's only fair
that I give you my percentage

20:12.220 --> 20:13.870
so you can get your house back.

20:13.938 --> 20:17.638
Quagmire, I don't know
what to say.

20:17.706 --> 20:18.771
Thank you.

20:18.839 --> 20:20.154
You're welcome.

20:20.222 --> 20:22.172
And if it makes you feel better,
it was money well spent.

20:22.240 --> 20:23.724
Just ask Sandra
the waitress over there.

20:23.791 --> 20:25.341
We had a great time
last night.

20:25.409 --> 20:26.974
PETER:
Tough girl.

20:27.042 --> 20:28.341
Made it into work.

20:31.311 --> 20:33.376
Boy, I got to say,
it's good to be home.

20:33.443 --> 20:35.211
Yeah. Despite all
the ups and downs,

20:35.279 --> 20:36.844
things turned out for the best.

20:36.912 --> 20:39.513
I mean, really, we're no worse
off than we were before.

20:39.580 --> 20:41.180
Plus, we learned something.

20:41.248 --> 20:43.014
And you can't
put a price on that.

20:43.081 --> 20:44.414
Look, Lois, we all know
what happened.

20:44.481 --> 20:45.614
We're all depressed.

20:45.666 --> 20:47.332
All right, let's just
go to different rooms

20:47.400 --> 20:49.233
and stare blankly
out the window, saying nothing.

20:49.234 --> 21:50.234
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru